banks financial planners are filling us  in on the concepts of anchoring and  status quo bias  in financial decision making here's the  information  [Music]  so status quo bias is the tendency that  we have to resist  change even if that change might be good  for us  so for example you might have a real  estate agent or a cpa  who you've had for quite some time and  even though they're not meeting your  expectations  you tend to stick with them because  they're familiar to you and you just  don't want to switch  or maybe you're the type of person who  whenever you go out to eat  you order the same thing every time  because if you try something new  you may not like it and it's just not  worth the risk so in essence status quo  bias is  maintaining the status quo so status quo  bias affects our financial decision  making in a number of ways so for  example you might have a position  in your portfolio that you've had for  quite some time  and maybe it's time to move on and sell  it and buy something else but because  you've had it for so long  you're kind of attached to it so you  really don't want to sell it or for  example maybe you've inherited an asset  like a stock or a piece of property from  a deceased relative and  rather than sell it and use the cash to  buy something else  you hang on to it because again you're  attached to it i always tell clients to  ask yourself this  if i had this much money in cash today  would i use it  to buy that asset every day that you  don't sell an asset  is a day that you've decided to buy it  so the best way that i've seen to combat  this bias or any other bias for that  matter is just to have another set of  eyes in the room you know  as financial advisors it's not just  about what stocks should i buy or when  should i retire  but it's about putting somebody else on  your side and and giving a different  perspective to your situation to help  you make better decisions with your  money  anchoring is a behavioral bias that  marketers have used on consumers to  influence them for years  when studies have been done on anchoring  researchers showed that subjects were  exposed to a higher number first  they value that item at a higher price  point and vice versa  effectively you can be convinced to pay  more for something without even  realizing it if you've ever walked into  a store and you've seen a price tag that  says 59.99  and then a sale price underneath it for  39.99 that's drawn to anchor you to the  higher price point so the sales price  seems like it's a great value so many  clients out there have  made an investment that at some point in  time is worth less than they originally  paid  and so oftentimes they will want to get  out of that investment with the caveat  that returns to its original value first  this would be because they are anchored  to that original purchase price so this  bias may inhibit you from actually  investing something that would perform  better over the years while waiting for  it to return its original value which  actually may never happen  some clients have fond memories of the  good old days when there are much higher  interest rates in the economy  so it impacts the way they view today's  low interest rates counterintuitively  they now view mortgage rates which were  much higher back then as great values  today  whereas some younger clients may not  have that anchor in time where they may  have only ever known a low interest rate  environment  so likewise consumers today who don't  have that past anchor have a different  expectation of what a good interest rate  looks like  for more information go to  www.nsbank.com  wfs big thanks to nevada state bank for  being one of our community pride  partners it's appearing that home or
 

